Zero-Knowledge Proof (ZKP) is a cornerstone in privacy-preserving computing, addressing critical challenges in domains such as finance and healthcare by ensuring data confidentiality during computation. However, the high computational overhead of ZKP, particularly in proof generation and verification, limits its scalability and usability in real-world applications. Existing efforts to accelerate ZKP primarily focus on specific components, such as polynomial commitment schemes or elliptic curve operations, but fail to deliver an integrated, flexible, and efficient end-to-end solution that includes witness generation on commercial computing platforms. In this work, we present MegaZK, a GPU-based end-to-end acceleration system targeting both time and memory efficiency for a Plonkish+FRI proof generation. MegaZK addresses three key challenges: (1) accelerating witness generation on GPU by designing Type-Aware Layered Scheduling and an output-aligned witness format that reorients data placement around execution layers to enable near-ideal memory coalescing; (2) supporting tunable memory footprint optimization by identifying and removing the duplicated information in low-degree extensions through in-place segmented computing, along with other optimizations; (3) achieving full GPU end-to-end proof generation, eliminating the data communication between the host and the device during proof generation. Experimental results on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU show on average 37.4 × end-to-end acceleration compared to state-of-the-art CPU implementations and on average 24.3 × speedup over existing GPU-based approaches, and 6.1 × speedup compared to state-of-the-art ASIC accelerator. The GPU memory usage is also reduced by 80.9%.